1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,840
Eleven forty at night,

2
00:00:01,840 --> 00:00:03,920
in the middle of the North Atlantic

3
00:00:03,920 --> 00:00:05,880
The largest ship in the world,

4
00:00:05,880 --> 00:00:07,720
the ship they called unsinkable,

5
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:09,480
hits an iceberg

6
00:00:10,321 --> 00:00:12,031
Less than three hours later,

7
00:00:12,031 --> 00:00:13,991
she is on the bottom of the sea

8
00:00:13,991 --> 00:00:16,791
This is the story of the Titanic's last night,

9
00:00:16,791 --> 00:00:18,551
minute by minute

10
00:00:25,283 --> 00:00:27,233
In the early twentieth century,

11
00:00:27,233 --> 00:00:29,593
British shipping lines fought hard for the

12
00:00:29,593 --> 00:00:30,673
Atlantic route,

13
00:00:30,673 --> 00:00:33,273
carrying people from Europe to America

14
00:00:35,332 --> 00:00:38,002
The White Star Line decided to build three

15
00:00:38,002 --> 00:00:39,122
giant ships,

16
00:00:39,122 --> 00:00:41,202
the most luxurious ever made

17
00:00:41,202 --> 00:00:43,802
The second one was named Titanic

18
00:00:45,860 --> 00:00:47,330
In nineteen oh nine,

19
00:00:47,330 --> 00:00:50,236
thousands of workers at the Harland and Wolff

20
00:00:50,236 --> 00:00:52,836
shipyard in Belfast got to work

21
00:00:55,434 --> 00:00:58,394
Titanic was about two hundred sixty-nine meters

22
00:00:58,394 --> 00:00:58,754
long,

23
00:00:58,754 --> 00:01:00,914
as tall as an eleven-story building,

24
00:01:00,914 --> 00:01:03,714
and the largest moving man-made object in the

25
00:01:03,714 --> 00:01:05,794
world at the time

26
00:01:07,842 --> 00:01:10,752
Her hull was divided into sixteen watertight

27
00:01:10,752 --> 00:01:11,792
compartments

28
00:01:11,792 --> 00:01:13,832
Even if the first four flooded,

29
00:01:13,832 --> 00:01:15,912
she would still float

30
00:01:18,214 --> 00:01:20,744
Newspapers called her practically unsinkable

31
00:01:20,744 --> 00:01:21,964
Over time,

32
00:01:21,964 --> 00:01:24,364
people dropped the word practically

33
00:01:26,441 --> 00:01:27,831
On May thirty-first,

34
00:01:27,831 --> 00:01:29,111
nineteen eleven,

35
00:01:29,111 --> 00:01:31,991
about ten thousand people watched the giant

36
00:01:31,991 --> 00:01:34,591
hull slide into the River Lagan

37
00:01:42,017 --> 00:01:43,767
At noon on April tenth,

38
00:01:43,767 --> 00:01:44,927
nineteen twelve,

39
00:01:44,927 --> 00:01:46,767
Titanic left Southampton,

40
00:01:46,767 --> 00:01:47,407
England,

41
00:01:47,407 --> 00:01:50,007
on her maiden voyage to New York

42
00:01:52,574 --> 00:01:53,764
As she left the dock,

43
00:01:53,764 --> 00:01:56,284
the pull of her giant hull snapped the mooring

44
00:01:56,284 --> 00:01:57,804
lines of another ship

45
00:01:57,804 --> 00:01:59,244
They nearly collided

46
00:01:59,244 --> 00:02:01,844
Many saw it as a bad omen

47
00:02:04,333 --> 00:02:06,683
She stopped at Cherbourg in France,

48
00:02:06,683 --> 00:02:08,563
then Queenstown in Ireland,

49
00:02:08,563 --> 00:02:10,963
to pick up more passengers

50
00:02:13,572 --> 00:02:15,202
When she headed out to sea,

51
00:02:15,202 --> 00:02:17,402
there were about two thousand two hundred

52
00:02:17,402 --> 00:02:18,482
people on board,

53
00:02:18,482 --> 00:02:20,242
passengers and crew

54
00:02:22,337 --> 00:02:24,607
In command was Captain Edward Smith,

55
00:02:24,607 --> 00:02:27,327
a veteran sailor who was said to be retiring

56
00:02:27,327 --> 00:02:29,087
after this voyage

57
00:02:31,617 --> 00:02:33,847
Also on board were Thomas Andrews,

58
00:02:33,847 --> 00:02:35,127
the ship's designer,

59
00:02:35,127 --> 00:02:36,327
and Bruce Ismay,

60
00:02:36,327 --> 00:02:38,927
chairman of the White Star Line

61
00:02:45,483 --> 00:02:47,513
Titanic was like a floating city,

62
00:02:47,513 --> 00:02:49,593
divided into three classes

63
00:02:51,847 --> 00:02:53,837
First class had a grand staircase,

64
00:02:53,837 --> 00:02:54,917
a swimming pool,

65
00:02:54,917 --> 00:02:55,517
a gym,

66
00:02:55,517 --> 00:02:56,677
a Turkish bath,

67
00:02:56,677 --> 00:02:58,437
and ten-course dinners

68
00:03:01,084 --> 00:03:03,994
The most luxurious first-class suite cost more

69
00:03:03,994 --> 00:03:06,594
than an ordinary worker earned in many years

70
00:03:09,926 --> 00:03:12,356
On the lower decks were more than seven hundred

71
00:03:12,356 --> 00:03:14,076
third-class passengers,

72
00:03:14,076 --> 00:03:15,556
mostly immigrants,

73
00:03:15,556 --> 00:03:18,076
carrying everything they owned to start a new

74
00:03:18,076 --> 00:03:19,836
life in America

75
00:03:22,316 --> 00:03:23,396
Deep in the ship,

76
00:03:23,396 --> 00:03:26,196
hundreds of stokers shoveled coal day and night

77
00:03:26,196 --> 00:03:28,596
to keep her running fast

78
00:03:30,963 --> 00:03:32,993
On deck there were twenty lifeboats,

79
00:03:32,993 --> 00:03:35,633
with room for only about one thousand one

80
00:03:35,633 --> 00:03:37,313
hundred seventy-eight people,

81
00:03:37,313 --> 00:03:39,913
less than half of everyone on board

82
00:03:42,179 --> 00:03:44,009
But that was perfectly legal

83
00:03:44,009 --> 00:03:46,249
The rules of the time were far too old

84
00:03:46,249 --> 00:03:48,649
for giant ships like Titanic

85
00:03:55,817 --> 00:03:57,207
On April fourteenth,

86
00:03:57,207 --> 00:04:00,407
Titanic's radio room received several warnings

87
00:04:00,407 --> 00:04:01,487
from other ships:

88
00:04:01,487 --> 00:04:03,567
there was ice ahead

89
00:04:05,205 --> 00:04:07,435
The two young radio operators,

90
00:04:07,435 --> 00:04:09,475
Jack Phillips and Harold Bride,

91
00:04:09,475 --> 00:04:12,315
were busy nonstop sending passengers' private

92
00:04:12,315 --> 00:04:13,435
messages

93
00:04:16,017 --> 00:04:18,287
Not every warning reached the bridge

94
00:04:18,287 --> 00:04:20,887
The ship kept going at about twenty-two knots

95
00:04:24,405 --> 00:04:25,755
Around eleven at night,

96
00:04:25,755 --> 00:04:28,595
the nearby ship Californian reported that it

97
00:04:28,595 --> 00:04:29,235
had stopped,

98
00:04:29,235 --> 00:04:30,995
surrounded by ice

99
00:04:33,015 --> 00:04:33,525
Phillips,

100
00:04:33,525 --> 00:04:34,845
busy with messages,

101
00:04:34,845 --> 00:04:37,845
cut them off and told them not to interrupt

102
00:04:37,845 --> 00:04:40,885
The Californian's radio operator switched off

103
00:04:40,885 --> 00:04:43,485
his set and went to bed

104
00:04:45,046 --> 00:04:46,556
There was no moon that night,

105
00:04:46,556 --> 00:04:48,796
and the sea was as flat as glass

106
00:04:48,796 --> 00:04:51,316
With no waves breaking at the base of the

107
00:04:51,316 --> 00:04:51,796
ice,

108
00:04:51,796 --> 00:04:54,396
icebergs were very hard to see

109
00:04:56,835 --> 00:04:58,105
Up in the crow's nest,

110
00:04:58,105 --> 00:05:00,665
the two lookouts had to rely on their eyes

111
00:05:00,665 --> 00:05:01,265
alone

112
00:05:01,265 --> 00:05:03,665
Their binoculars were not there

113
00:05:11,083 --> 00:05:13,273
At about eleven forty at night,

114
00:05:13,273 --> 00:05:16,113
lookout Frederick Fleet saw a huge dark shape

115
00:05:16,113 --> 00:05:17,873
looming straight ahead

116
00:05:19,555 --> 00:05:21,865
He rang the bell three times and called the

117
00:05:21,865 --> 00:05:22,465
bridge:

118
00:05:22,465 --> 00:05:23,065
iceberg,

119
00:05:23,065 --> 00:05:24,505
right ahead!

120
00:05:26,154 --> 00:05:28,504
Officer William Murdoch ordered the ship to

121
00:05:28,504 --> 00:05:30,664
turn and the engines reversed

122
00:05:30,664 --> 00:05:32,704
The giant ship began to swing,

123
00:05:32,704 --> 00:05:34,464
but too slowly

124
00:05:36,744 --> 00:05:39,434
Only about thirty-seven seconds later,

125
00:05:39,434 --> 00:05:41,974
Titanic's starboard side scraped along the

126
00:05:41,974 --> 00:05:43,094
iceberg

127
00:05:45,708 --> 00:05:48,618
Many passengers felt only a slight shudder

128
00:05:48,618 --> 00:05:51,338
Some even played with chunks of ice that had

129
00:05:51,338 --> 00:05:53,418
fallen on the deck

130
00:05:55,055 --> 00:05:56,445
But below the waterline,

131
00:05:56,445 --> 00:05:58,605
the hull had been opened along several

132
00:05:58,605 --> 00:05:59,325
compartments

133
00:05:59,325 --> 00:06:01,925
Seawater poured into the first five

134
00:06:05,250 --> 00:06:07,640
Thomas Andrews went down to inspect,

135
00:06:07,640 --> 00:06:09,160
then told the captain:

136
00:06:09,160 --> 00:06:10,880
the ship would certainly sink,

137
00:06:10,880 --> 00:06:13,480
in maybe one to two hours

138
00:06:20,717 --> 00:06:21,947
Just after midnight,

139
00:06:21,947 --> 00:06:24,587
Captain Smith ordered the lifeboats uncovered

140
00:06:24,587 --> 00:06:27,187
and the passengers brought up on deck

141
00:06:29,856 --> 00:06:32,126
He knew something most people did not:

142
00:06:32,126 --> 00:06:34,726
there was not enough room for everyone

143
00:06:36,955 --> 00:06:38,105
The order was given:

144
00:06:38,105 --> 00:06:40,185
women and children first

145
00:06:42,950 --> 00:06:45,460
But many people did not believe the ship would

146
00:06:45,460 --> 00:06:45,980
sink

147
00:06:45,980 --> 00:06:48,100
They did not want to leave the warm,

148
00:06:48,100 --> 00:06:50,660
bright ship for a small boat in the freezing

149
00:06:50,660 --> 00:06:51,780
night

150
00:06:53,689 --> 00:06:56,239
The first lifeboat to leave carried only about

151
00:06:56,239 --> 00:06:57,479
twenty-eight people,

152
00:06:57,479 --> 00:07:00,079
though it had room for sixty-five

153
00:07:02,617 --> 00:07:05,447
Many third-class passengers on the lowest decks

154
00:07:05,447 --> 00:07:07,847
had to find their way through long corridors

155
00:07:07,847 --> 00:07:10,447
and narrow stairs to reach the deck

156
00:07:13,008 --> 00:07:13,638
Meanwhile,

157
00:07:13,638 --> 00:07:15,238
the water kept rising

158
00:07:15,238 --> 00:07:16,598
The bow sank lower,

159
00:07:16,598 --> 00:07:18,358
little by little

160
00:07:25,383 --> 00:07:26,573
In the radio room,

161
00:07:26,573 --> 00:07:29,493
Phillips kept tapping out the distress call

162
00:07:29,493 --> 00:07:30,093
CQD,

163
00:07:30,093 --> 00:07:32,019
then switched to the new signal,

164
00:07:32,019 --> 00:07:33,139
SOS

165
00:07:36,455 --> 00:07:36,815
The ship Carpathia,

166
00:07:36,815 --> 00:07:38,935
about a hundred kilometers away,

167
00:07:38,935 --> 00:07:39,855
heard the call

168
00:07:39,855 --> 00:07:42,655
Captain Arthur Rostron immediately turned his

169
00:07:42,655 --> 00:07:44,415
ship toward Titanic

170
00:07:47,080 --> 00:07:49,270
He shut off the heating to send every bit

171
00:07:49,270 --> 00:07:50,950
of steam to the engines,

172
00:07:50,950 --> 00:07:53,470
and raced faster than ever through the ice

173
00:07:53,470 --> 00:07:55,550
field in the dark

174
00:07:57,346 --> 00:08:00,696
But Carpathia needed almost four hours to get

175
00:08:00,696 --> 00:08:01,056
there

176
00:08:01,056 --> 00:08:03,656
Titanic did not have that much time

177
00:08:06,097 --> 00:08:06,727
Meanwhile,

178
00:08:06,727 --> 00:08:09,767
the Californian was only a few dozen kilometers

179
00:08:09,767 --> 00:08:10,207
away

180
00:08:10,207 --> 00:08:12,807
Its radio had been switched off

181
00:08:14,860 --> 00:08:17,460
Sailors on the Californian saw white rockets

182
00:08:17,460 --> 00:08:18,860
going up into the sky,

183
00:08:18,860 --> 00:08:21,260
but did not understand that they were calls for

184
00:08:21,260 --> 00:08:22,380
help

185
00:08:29,717 --> 00:08:31,187
Close to two in the morning,

186
00:08:31,187 --> 00:08:33,507
the last lifeboats left the ship

187
00:08:33,507 --> 00:08:36,427
More than one thousand five hundred people were

188
00:08:36,427 --> 00:08:38,187
still on board

189
00:08:40,134 --> 00:08:41,044
The ship's band,

190
00:08:41,044 --> 00:08:42,564
led by Wallace Hartley,

191
00:08:42,564 --> 00:08:45,164
kept playing on deck to keep everyone calm

192
00:08:48,376 --> 00:08:50,126
Isidor and Ida Straus,

193
00:08:50,126 --> 00:08:52,966
owners of a famous New York department store,

194
00:08:52,966 --> 00:08:54,806
refused to be separated

195
00:08:54,806 --> 00:08:57,126
She gave her seat in the lifeboat to her

196
00:08:57,126 --> 00:08:59,726
maid and stayed with her husband

197
00:09:02,073 --> 00:09:05,263
Radio operator Jack Phillips kept working until

198
00:09:05,263 --> 00:09:07,863
the captain told everyone to save themselves

199
00:09:10,926 --> 00:09:13,726
Thomas Andrews was last seen in the first-class

200
00:09:13,726 --> 00:09:14,726
smoking room

201
00:09:14,726 --> 00:09:17,326
Captain Smith stayed with his ship to the end

202
00:09:20,655 --> 00:09:21,165
On deck,

203
00:09:21,165 --> 00:09:23,325
many men gave up their places,

204
00:09:23,325 --> 00:09:25,965
helped women and children into the boats,

205
00:09:25,965 --> 00:09:28,365
then quietly stood and waited

206
00:09:36,150 --> 00:09:38,420
The bow sank deep under the water

207
00:09:38,420 --> 00:09:41,020
The huge stern slowly rose into the night sky

208
00:09:44,746 --> 00:09:46,696
The lights flickered a few times,

209
00:09:46,696 --> 00:09:49,096
then went out for good

210
00:09:51,323 --> 00:09:52,793
Unable to bear the weight,

211
00:09:52,793 --> 00:09:55,193
the hull broke in two

212
00:09:57,368 --> 00:09:58,958
At two twenty in the morning,

213
00:09:58,958 --> 00:10:01,238
the stern disappeared beneath the sea

214
00:10:01,238 --> 00:10:03,518
From the collision to the sinking took only

215
00:10:03,518 --> 00:10:06,118
about two hours and forty minutes

216
00:10:08,831 --> 00:10:11,101
The water that night was about minus two

217
00:10:11,101 --> 00:10:12,541
degrees Celsius

218
00:10:12,541 --> 00:10:15,021
Most people who ended up in the water could

219
00:10:15,021 --> 00:10:17,101
not survive for long

220
00:10:18,889 --> 00:10:20,959
Very few lifeboats went back

221
00:10:20,959 --> 00:10:23,519
Officer Harold Lowe moved his passengers to

222
00:10:23,519 --> 00:10:24,399
other boats,

223
00:10:24,399 --> 00:10:26,999
then rowed back and saved a few more people

224
00:10:29,762 --> 00:10:31,472
Then the sea fell silent

225
00:10:31,472 --> 00:10:34,032
The people in the boats could only wait,

226
00:10:34,032 --> 00:10:36,632
in the dark and the cold

227
00:10:43,917 --> 00:10:45,547
At around four in the morning,

228
00:10:45,547 --> 00:10:47,827
the people in the boats saw a flare on

229
00:10:47,827 --> 00:10:48,275
the horizon

230
00:10:48,275 --> 00:10:50,035
Carpathia had arrived

231
00:10:53,424 --> 00:10:54,294
One by one,

232
00:10:54,294 --> 00:10:56,134
the lifeboats were brought aboard

233
00:10:56,134 --> 00:10:57,934
By after eight in the morning,

234
00:10:57,934 --> 00:11:00,534
the last survivors were on the ship

235
00:11:03,062 --> 00:11:05,772
About seven hundred ten people survived

236
00:11:05,772 --> 00:11:08,372
More than one thousand five hundred died

237
00:11:11,816 --> 00:11:13,526
Survival was very unequal:

238
00:11:13,526 --> 00:11:16,526
most first-class women and children were saved,

239
00:11:16,526 --> 00:11:19,126
while most third-class men were not

240
00:11:21,710 --> 00:11:23,532
On the evening of April eighteenth,

241
00:11:23,532 --> 00:11:26,820
Carpathia arrived in New York in the rain

242
00:11:26,820 --> 00:11:29,420
Tens of thousands of people waited in silence

243
00:11:33,213 --> 00:11:35,923
Captain Rostron was hailed as a hero

244
00:11:35,923 --> 00:11:38,443
He only said he had done his duty as

245
00:11:38,443 --> 00:11:39,883
a sailor

246
00:11:46,717 --> 00:11:49,947
America and Britain opened two major inquiries

247
00:11:49,947 --> 00:11:51,707
into the disaster

248
00:11:53,114 --> 00:11:55,424
Captain Stanley Lord of the Californian was

249
00:11:55,424 --> 00:11:58,024
heavily criticized for not coming to help,

250
00:11:58,024 --> 00:12:00,504
even though his crew had seen the rockets

251
00:12:00,504 --> 00:12:02,904
He later lost his job

252
00:12:04,988 --> 00:12:06,308
Bruce Ismay survived,

253
00:12:06,308 --> 00:12:08,908
but newspapers called him a coward for getting

254
00:12:08,908 --> 00:12:11,508
into a lifeboat while women and children were

255
00:12:11,508 --> 00:12:13,268
still on board

256
00:12:15,477 --> 00:12:16,907
In nineteen fourteen,

257
00:12:16,907 --> 00:12:19,467
nations signed the first International

258
00:12:19,467 --> 00:12:21,987
Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea,

259
00:12:21,987 --> 00:12:23,747
known as SOLAS

260
00:12:25,743 --> 00:12:27,013
Under the new rules,

261
00:12:27,013 --> 00:12:29,613
every ship had to carry enough lifeboats for

262
00:12:29,613 --> 00:12:30,213
everyone,

263
00:12:30,213 --> 00:12:31,693
hold lifeboat drills,

264
00:12:31,693 --> 00:12:34,293
and keep a radio watch twenty-four hours a day

265
00:12:37,684 --> 00:12:40,914
An International Ice Patrol was also created to

266
00:12:40,914 --> 00:12:43,434
track icebergs in the North Atlantic

267
00:12:43,434 --> 00:12:45,514
It still operates today

268
00:12:53,283 --> 00:12:54,713
For seventy-three years,

269
00:12:54,713 --> 00:12:57,313
no one knew exactly where Titanic lay

270
00:12:59,861 --> 00:13:01,491
In nineteen eighty-five,

271
00:13:01,491 --> 00:13:04,131
oceanographer Robert Ballard and his French

272
00:13:04,131 --> 00:13:06,691
colleague Jean-Louis Michel began searching

273
00:13:06,691 --> 00:13:09,291
with an underwater robot towed behind a ship

274
00:13:12,357 --> 00:13:14,547
It was later revealed that the trip was

275
00:13:14,547 --> 00:13:17,747
actually a secret US Navy mission to find two

276
00:13:17,747 --> 00:13:19,707
sunken nuclear submarines

277
00:13:19,707 --> 00:13:22,307
Titanic was only the cover story

278
00:13:24,748 --> 00:13:26,138
On September first,

279
00:13:26,138 --> 00:13:27,698
nineteen eighty-five,

280
00:13:27,698 --> 00:13:29,898
their camera spotted a giant boiler

281
00:13:29,898 --> 00:13:32,938
Titanic lay about three thousand eight hundred

282
00:13:32,938 --> 00:13:33,738
meters down,

283
00:13:33,738 --> 00:13:35,498
broken in two

284
00:13:37,620 --> 00:13:38,050
Today,

285
00:13:38,050 --> 00:13:40,930
the wreck is being eaten away by rust and

286
00:13:40,930 --> 00:13:42,570
bacteria every day

287
